    "narrative": "Today\u2019s optimal lineup for Ghost Ride the WHIP is anchored by **Josh Naylor** (72.69) and **Vinnie Pasquantino** (70.55), both of whom benefit significantly from facing favorable opposing arms. **Josh Naylor** capitalizes on a high-ceiling matchup against Jeffrey Springs, where park factors and the pitcher\u2019s projected xERA create a prime environment for run production. Similarly, **Vinnie Pasquantino** draws a +14% boost from a strong platoon advantage against Sonny Gray, making him our safest bet for high-efficiency RBI output. **James Wood** remains a priority start at 68.40; while he faces Yusei Kikuchi, his elite xwOBA and a +10% boost from the leadoff spot solidify his value despite the moderate pitching matchup.\n\nOur strategy continues to prioritize stolen base potential as a scarce resource. **Corbin Carroll** (65.51) and **Trevor Story** (66.75) are both recommended starts, largely due to their ability to exploit opposing battery vulnerabilities. **Corbin Carroll** gets a +4% bump thanks to the pitcher's slow delivery, while **Trevor Story** maintains strong efficiency in the #5 hole. The most difficult personnel decision involved **Max Muncy**, who finished just outside the starting nine with a 55.21 score. Despite a generous park factor, his raw projection and platoon dynamics did not out-leverage the specific situational boosts of **Gabriel Moreno** or **Austin Riley**, the latter of whom earns his keep today through a dominant 65 PA BvP history against Aaron Nola.\n\nOwners should exercise caution regarding the high volume of \"Lineup Pending\" statuses. We have modeled projections for **Gabriel Moreno**, **Brice Turang**, **Cole Young**, **Austin Riley**, **Corbin Carroll**, **Griffin Conine**, **Jose Caballero**, and **Josh Naylor** based on historical spot trends, but any unexpected drop in the batting order for **Cole Young** or **Jose Caballero** could render these efficiency scores volatile. Specifically, **Jose Caballero** sits on the low end of our starting threshold at 43.34; if his official lineup spot moves lower than #9, he will be an immediate candidate for benching to preserve our game caps.",
